> [3]: Trapdoors should always come out in the same place, if that's
> codeable. It makes sense. -ekiM

Nice idea, although a little tricky to implement.  When a level is left
for a new one, the old level is packed up into a temporary file and the
new one either generated anew or loaded from disk.  The hole's
destination can't be generated until the new level is made, but that
can't be done with the old level in memory, and it has to still be
around for Nethack to add the hole's destination to the trap structure.

Once in a while I develop an unreasonable fantasy to make Nethack's
levels spatially contiguous with each other, so a hole dug in a level
would, if possible, lead to the same X Y coordinates on the level
below, but that would require considerable changes to the game and is
likely not feasible.  Doing that would make your idea moot, but I don't
think it'll happen any time soon.

> [4]: Acid resistance is meaningless.  Add a couple monsters (say, a
> purple S) that spit acid for 4d10 or something, so that acid resistance
> might be worth considering -ekiM

There's a type of dragon that spits acid, but I know what you're
talking about.  Note, however, that acid resistance cannot be gained
from eating monsters, and it only has a couple of ways to acquire it
from equipment, one of 'em containing the letters D, S and M.  Still, I
like the idea.

> [5]: Zapping wands of digging <: A way to make holes in the ceiling,
> such that you can levitate up through them. -workimer

One big obstacle I can see to this involves the interface: how do you
*represent* a hole in the ceiling, when anything can be on the ground
beneath it?  Also, this could make it way too easy to get the Amulet
back to the surface, that would have to be taken into consideration.

> [6]: If teleporting monsters can't make it to the upstair, they
> teleport to the downstair. If they can't make it there, they teleport a
> long way from you to heal up. They're supposed to be supreme evil
> opponents, not klutzes who get outfoxed by a boulder on their escape
> route. There might have to be a longer tele-timeout to balance this.
> -ekiM

I *really* like the idea of monsters with teleport and telecontrol
mixing up their tactics, they're supposed to be too wily a class of
opponent to all be beaten the same way.  Indeed, the best way to handle
these jokers is to stand on the downstair, wait until the teleport to
you to attack, then change levels.  They'll usualy follow you down, and
then you'll be standing on the up stairs, ready to smack 'em.  I think
this will probably be added to the game eventually, maybe in the next
major version.

It is possible to kill a monster before he teleports away now if you
have the right equipment, and sometimes it happens purely on accident,
so what would have to be done is to make those methods of killing the
foe easier and/or quicker.  You can do it with weapons that deliver
instant kills, wands of death in some circumstances, you could hold the
monster in place, use a cockatrice corpse (or poly into a cockatrice
oneself in some cases), polymorph into a black dragon and breathe, get
a pet purple worm to engulf him, drain him with Stormbringer if he's
not drain-resistant, or just deliver enough damage with a weapon in one
turn to overcome the monster's sense of peril and get him to zero HP.
Some can also be bribed right out of the game.

If these monsters are made harder to kill, then these tactics (and
others I have probably neglected) will become more important.  So, we'd
have to make two or three of them easier to compensate.

